#!/usr/bin/python3 -IS
"""Run the standard version a project pins, from outside its checkout.

Install once per machine with the line in the README, which downloads a
released copy of this file, checks its SHA-256 and runs `self-install`:

    ballast self-install [--dir DIR] [--force]   # install this file as DIR/ballast
    ballast --version
    ballast doctor [--json]    # read-only check of every prerequisite

To adopt a repository, from its root (blank or established):

    ballast init [--ref REF] [--description TEXT] [--stack STACK]

`init` is the only command that runs before a pin exists. It pins REF (the
existing pin on a rerun, else this CLI's own release), fetches that version
and runs its tools/init, which writes only absent project files, proposes
every other change as an ignored patch, installs through setup and ends with
`ballast trust` as the operator's next action.

Then, from a project root whose ballast.toml has `[standard] ref = "..."`:

    ballast setup              # fetch that version, run its tools/setup
    ballast preview REF [--json]   # what moving the pin to REF would do
    ballast trust              # after reviewing the checkout
    ballast run start|resume ...
    ballast ledger ...
    ballast intake ...         # the feature-intake helper

Each version is extracted once into $XDG_DATA_HOME/ballast/standard/<ref>/,
under a per-version lock, and published together with a record of its content;
`setup` and `preview` verify a cached copy against that record and fetch a
damaged one again. Only `setup` and `preview` download; every other command
runs the launcher of an already fetched version. The first `run`, `ledger` or
`intake` in a new worktree prepares its installation from a verified
installation on this machine, without downloading. `preview` runs the target
version's own setup in a disposable project and changes nothing else. The
repository is fixed here, so a project can choose a version but never a
source. Set BALLAST_STANDARD_DIR to a local checkout of this repository to
develop the standard itself; from such a checkout,
`install -m 0755 tools/ballast ~/.local/bin/ballast` also installs it.
`doctor` writes nothing, downloads nothing and runs only the read-only probes a
fetched version declares in its tools/cli.toml.
"""

